WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.078
In 2013, a Missouri jury found Russ Faria
guilty of murdering his wife.

00:00:07.418 --> 00:00:10.229
He was sentenced to life in prison without
parole.

00:00:10.569 --> 00:00:13.709
Two years later, a judge found him not
guilty.

00:00:14.209 --> 00:00:17.989
Today, prosecutors have charged someone
else with the murder:

00:00:17.989 --> 00:00:20.923
the friend who drove his wife home that
night.

00:00:21.263 --> 00:00:26.116
Days before the killing, that friend had
been made the sole beneficiary of the

00:00:26.116 --> 00:00:28.774
victim's $150,000 life insurance policy.

00:00:31.714 --> 00:00:34.459
So how did a man with four alibi
witnesses,

00:00:34.459 --> 00:00:37.932
a receipt and a phone trail end up with a
life sentence?

00:00:38.272 --> 00:00:42.646
And why did it take nearly ten years to
charge the last person known to have seen

00:00:42.646 --> 00:00:43.646
her alive?

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:53.429
December 27th, 2011. Troy, Missouri, a
small town about an hour northwest of St.

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:54.429
Louis.

00:00:54.743 --> 00:00:58.644
Betsy Faria was 42, a mother of two
daughters.

00:00:58.984 --> 00:01:02.686
She was also fighting cancer that had
spread to her liver. That day,

00:01:02.686 --> 00:01:04.137
she'd had chemotherapy.

00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:07.424
That evening, her friend Pam Hupp drove
her home.

00:01:07.764 --> 00:01:11.840
According to later reporting, Hupp had
insisted on giving her the ride.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.565
Betsy's husband, Russ, was somewhere else
entirely:

00:01:15.565 --> 00:01:20.495
at his regular Tuesday game night with
four friends, more than 20 miles away.

00:01:20.835 --> 00:01:24.627
On his way home, he stopped at an Arby's.
There was a receipt,

00:01:24.627 --> 00:01:26.244
and surveillance video.

00:01:26.584 --> 00:01:30.189
At 9:40 that night, Russ called 911.

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:33.301
He had found Betsy dead inside their home.

00:01:33.641 --> 00:01:36.346
She had been stabbed 55 times.

00:01:37.146 --> 00:01:42.328
Days later, after Betsy's funeral, Russ
was arrested and charged with first-degree

00:01:42.328 --> 00:01:43.328
murder.

00:01:45.634 --> 00:01:48.467
Prosecutors built their case around the
husband.

00:01:48.807 --> 00:01:52.940
They pointed to a pair of slippers they
said were stained with blood.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.413
They told jurors his friends were covering
for him,

00:01:56.413 --> 00:01:58.996
and that his frantic 911 call was an act.

00:01:59.336 --> 00:02:02.661
But a different set of facts never reached
that jury.

00:02:03.261 --> 00:02:09.034
Days before she died, Betsy had changed
the beneficiary of her $150,000 life

00:02:09.034 --> 00:02:12.272
insurance policy, from Russ to Pam Hupp.

00:02:12.612 --> 00:02:16.144
Hupp said Betsy wanted the money to go to
her daughters one day.

00:02:16.484 --> 00:02:19.254
Hupp's own account of that evening also
kept changing.

00:02:19.594 --> 00:02:23.756
She first told investigators she hadn't
gone inside the house. Later,

00:02:23.756 --> 00:02:27.142
that she'd gone as far as the living room.
Later still,

00:02:27.142 --> 00:02:29.195
that she'd been in the bedroom.

00:02:29.535 --> 00:02:34.232
Phone records showed her making a call at
7:04 p.m. from near the Faria home,

00:02:34.232 --> 00:02:37.627
and those records would become a key point
of dispute.

00:02:37.967 --> 00:02:42.536
But at Russ's first trial, the judge did
not allow the defense to argue that Hupp

00:02:42.536 --> 00:02:43.902
could be the killer.

00:02:44.242 --> 00:02:49.006
Missouri law required evidence directly
connecting another suspect to the crime,

00:02:49.006 --> 00:02:52.164
and the judge ruled that standard had not
been met.

00:02:52.504 --> 00:02:58.407
On November 21st, 2013, the jury found
Russ Faria guilty.

00:02:58.747 --> 00:03:03.263
The sentence: life in prison without
parole, plus 30 years.

00:03:06.363 --> 00:03:08.592
The insurance payout went to Pam Hupp.

00:03:08.932 --> 00:03:11.614
Betsy's daughters sued for it. They lost.

00:03:12.114 --> 00:03:14.462
An appeals court said the form named Hupp,

00:03:14.462 --> 00:03:18.766
and that Betsy's intentions beyond that
could not be determined from the evidence.

00:03:21.306 --> 00:03:24.160
Russ's lawyer, Joel Schwartz, kept
fighting.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:29.802
In 2015, the case went back to the trial
court, and on June 5th,

00:03:29.802 --> 00:03:36.315
Judge Steven Ohmer, a St. Louis judge
assigned to the case, ordered a new trial.

00:03:36.655 --> 00:03:39.278
His ruling centered on the evidence about
Pam Hupp.

00:03:39.618 --> 00:03:42.785
Days later, Russ walked out of jail on
bond,

00:03:42.785 --> 00:03:46.143
after about three and a half years behind
bars.

00:03:46.483 --> 00:03:52.764
In November 2015, he was tried again. This
time, he chose to have a judge decide,

00:03:52.764 --> 00:03:53.991
not a jury.

00:03:54.331 --> 00:03:57.303
And this time, the defense could point to
Pam Hupp.

00:03:57.643 --> 00:04:03.391
On November 6th, 2015, Judge Ohmer found
Russ Faria not guilty.

00:04:03.991 --> 00:04:08.037
And the judge openly criticized the
investigation that had put him in prison.

00:04:10.677 --> 00:04:17.616
Nine months later, in August 2016, Pam
Hupp called 911 from her home in O'Fallon,

00:04:17.616 --> 00:04:18.812
Missouri.

00:04:19.152 --> 00:04:23.183
She had shot and killed a man: Louis
Gumpenberger, 33,

00:04:23.183 --> 00:04:25.467
who lived with a disability.

00:04:25.807 --> 00:04:29.755
She told police he was an intruder, and
that he'd demanded "Russ's money."

00:04:30.095 --> 00:04:33.996
In his pocket, police found $900 in cash
and a note:

00:04:33.996 --> 00:04:38.436
apparent instructions to kidnap Hupp and
get "Russ's money."

00:04:38.776 --> 00:04:41.814
Prosecutors said the cash and the note had
been planted,

00:04:41.814 --> 00:04:45.833
and that the serial numbers ran in
sequence with bills found in Hupp's home.

00:04:46.173 --> 00:04:51.587
They said she had lured him by posing as a
producer for NBC's Dateline,

00:04:51.587 --> 00:04:55.033
offering $1,000 to help reenact a 911
call.

00:04:55.373 --> 00:04:59.739
Days earlier, another woman had reported
the same kind of approach.

00:05:00.079 --> 00:05:05.197
Prosecutors alleged it was an attempt to
frame Russ Faria all over again.

00:05:05.797 --> 00:05:11.432
In 2019, Hupp entered an Alford plea to
first-degree murder: not admitting guilt,

00:05:11.432 --> 00:05:15.642
but conceding the state had enough
evidence to convict her.

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:18.513
She was sentenced to life without parole.

00:05:18.853 --> 00:05:23.563
Investigators also took another look at a
death from 2013.

00:05:23.903 --> 00:05:28.448
Hupp's mother, Shirley Neumann, had died
after falling from her balcony at a senior

00:05:28.448 --> 00:05:29.571
living facility.

00:05:29.911 --> 00:05:33.180
Her death was ruled an accident. In 2017,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:38.258
the medical examiner changed the manner of
death to "undetermined."

00:05:38.598 --> 00:05:40.700
No one has been charged in her death.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:49.161
In 2020, Russ Faria settled his civil
rights lawsuit for $2 million,

00:05:49.161 --> 00:05:51.965
with no admission of wrongdoing.

00:05:52.305 --> 00:05:58.291
Then, in July 2021, Lincoln County
Prosecutor Mike Wood charged Pam Hupp with

00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:01.825
first-degree murder in Betsy Faria's
death.

00:06:02.165 --> 00:06:03.678
She pleaded not guilty.

00:06:04.018 --> 00:06:08.346
Wood also opened an investigation into how
the original case was handled.

00:06:08.686 --> 00:06:13.370
In 2025, a former sheriff's captain on the
case, Mike Merkel,

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:18.123
was charged with perjury over his
testimony in the Faria case.

00:06:18.123 --> 00:06:22.038
He is presumed innocent, and that case is
pending.

00:06:22.378 --> 00:06:26.840
The original prosecutor, Leah Askey, has
denied any wrongdoing.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.603
Hupp's trial has been moved and delayed
again and again.

00:06:30.943 --> 00:06:35.325
Her lawyers have pointed to DNA from an
unidentified man on evidence,

00:06:35.325 --> 00:06:39.591
including the knife handle. Prosecutors
dispute that it clears her.

00:06:39.931 --> 00:06:43.454
In April 2026, Hupp waived her right to a
jury,

00:06:43.454 --> 00:06:47.508
and the state dropped its pursuit of the
death penalty.

00:06:47.848 --> 00:06:53.398
A judge is scheduled to hear the case in
January 2028.

00:06:56.098 --> 00:07:00.757
Russ Faria spent about three and a half
years locked up for a murder he was

00:07:00.757 --> 00:07:02.483
ultimately acquitted of.

00:07:02.823 --> 00:07:07.707
Pam Hupp is serving life for killing Louis
Gumpenberger. In Betsy's case,

00:07:07.707 --> 00:07:11.354
she is charged, not convicted, and
presumed innocent.

00:07:11.694 --> 00:07:14.398
Betsy Faria was 42 years old.

00:07:15.198 --> 00:07:20.002
Nearly fifteen years after her death, no
court has decided who killed her.
